1
00:00:20,572 --> 00:00:29,059
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so, the subject I thought it would be good to speak about, comes down to a type of

2
00:00:31,422 --> 00:00:32,884
[SPEAKER_01]: To call it a view might be misleading.

3
00:00:32,925 --> 00:00:44,864
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like a kind of axiom of thought that is really taken for granted, that can be expressed in a few different ways, but it comes down to like the idea that

4
00:00:46,528 --> 00:00:54,155
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no such thing as certainty in regard to, really, the nature of things.

5
00:00:54,595 --> 00:00:58,919
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no such thing as, sort of, absolute knowledge.

6
00:00:59,139 --> 00:00:59,820
[SPEAKER_01]: And it will come...

7
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:03,263
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not usually something that people will state, like, explicitly.

8
00:01:03,343 --> 00:01:04,024
[SPEAKER_00]: It's implied.

9
00:01:04,204 --> 00:01:07,847
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's implied in their thoughts, their ideas.

10
00:01:08,808 --> 00:01:21,336
[SPEAKER_01]: And sometimes, you know, you'll be having a conversation with somebody about, for example, right and wrong, or something, and just when it seems like it's starting to go somewhere, then they say, oh, well, you can never really know, can you?

11
00:01:23,197 --> 00:01:23,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

12
00:01:24,138 --> 00:01:26,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's almost like the agnosticism in the suttas.

13
00:01:28,932 --> 00:01:39,759
[SPEAKER_00]: People have like a... what they deem to be sort of like a conventional knowledge, but then if you start pushing it towards the further clarity, it's all... it's taken as valid.

14
00:01:39,839 --> 00:01:42,061
[SPEAKER_00]: This kind of dismissal of, well, you can't really know.

15
00:01:43,102 --> 00:01:46,584
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's... but it's like, well, you obviously seem to think that you know that.

16
00:01:47,865 --> 00:01:50,026
[SPEAKER_00]: You obviously seem to think that you know that you can't really know.

17
00:01:50,146 --> 00:01:53,889
[SPEAKER_00]: But, okay, so if your premise is true, how do you know that?

18
00:01:54,289 --> 00:01:55,510
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you know that you can't know?

19
00:01:56,631 --> 00:01:58,552
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you realize it's a contradiction in terms, yeah.

20
00:01:59,963 --> 00:02:01,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so that's one thing.

21
00:02:01,144 --> 00:02:08,769
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, the statement, in order to be true, must be false, because if you apply it to itself.

22
00:02:09,649 --> 00:02:19,216
[SPEAKER_01]: And about this, there is a few things that I kind of wanted to bring up about it, because it's...

23
00:02:26,644 --> 00:02:30,375
[SPEAKER_01]: There's really something that a lot of people seem to subscribe to and it's like...

24
00:02:32,267 --> 00:02:54,701
[SPEAKER_01]: You can see that if you kind of take the the notion of knowledge in a kind of scientific sense, as in we need to know things about the world by gathering information and data about them, then you do have to say, well, whatever we can find out has to be, you know, it can always be disproved by something you never can absolutely know.

25
00:02:55,242 --> 00:02:57,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Because why do you think that is?

26
00:02:58,949 --> 00:03:05,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the data that you can gather and the information that you can gather depends on the instrument through which you are...

27
00:03:05,654 --> 00:03:06,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

28
00:03:06,575 --> 00:03:14,562
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's purely observational based on the senses, which means all you need to do in order to disprove it is find something, observe something different.

29
00:03:14,822 --> 00:03:15,983
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

30
00:03:16,863 --> 00:03:22,348
[SPEAKER_00]: And the even bigger problem, like in itself, as a method of learning about the world, that's fine, but

31
00:03:24,008 --> 00:03:40,693
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mind is not an object of your senses, cannot be the object of your senses, yet if your only criteria for learning and understanding is gathering information through the observation on the basis of your senses, means the entire domain of your mind and mental development is absolutely excluded from any recognition.

32
00:03:42,757 --> 00:03:50,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and it occurred to me that that also is, you know, it's not something that's exclusive to science.

33
00:03:50,220 --> 00:04:06,327
[SPEAKER_01]: In that way, I mean, the same type of basing one's ideas, one's views on this kind of information that one gathers, I mean, in, let's say, you know that today, I think it's Brahmajala Siddha, the DM, where he's like going through all of these...

34
00:04:06,827 --> 00:04:09,147
[SPEAKER_01]: These are views that different people can have.

35
00:04:09,687 --> 00:04:16,249
[SPEAKER_01]: And they have a huge variety of views based on, like, different types of information that they gather.

36
00:04:16,369 --> 00:04:21,950
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you have one who's been able to remember 40 eons of past lives or something.

37
00:04:21,970 --> 00:04:25,690
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's, like, unbelievable amount of information that he has.

38
00:04:26,250 --> 00:04:30,791
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, the problem is, like, that he then goes to say, well, that's the information I have.

39
00:04:30,851 --> 00:04:32,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, that's how the world is.

40
00:04:32,191 --> 00:04:33,672
[SPEAKER_01]: The world in itself is eternal.

41
00:04:33,912 --> 00:04:34,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

42
00:04:38,305 --> 00:04:48,409
[SPEAKER_01]: And the other thing I wanted to bring up about it is that it is one way of expressing what... the wrong view, it seems to me.

43
00:04:48,429 --> 00:04:58,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the... when he says there are no monks and brahmins who know the world and understand the nature of the world and declare it.

44
00:04:58,313 --> 00:05:05,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Like that... that certainty, direct knowledge, eh... is not a possibility.

45
00:05:07,557 --> 00:05:07,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Eh...

46
00:05:11,214 --> 00:05:15,184
[SPEAKER_01]: And, to me, that also seems like a...

47
00:05:19,002 --> 00:05:22,843
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, that's one aspect of this wrong view that the Buddha describes.

48
00:05:23,043 --> 00:05:30,545
[SPEAKER_01]: But when I think about it, I can see that it also implies the rest of that description of the wrong view.

49
00:05:31,105 --> 00:05:42,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're saying, when somebody says, like I'm talking to somebody and they say, well, you can never really know, you never have any certainty, then, you know, it means for them that

50
00:05:44,290 --> 00:05:53,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, whatever I might do, whatever I might kind of strive for, I'm never going to ever know for sure whether it's right or wrong.

51
00:05:54,018 --> 00:05:59,243
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm never going to know for sure, like, what's the result of it.

52
00:05:59,283 --> 00:06:03,827
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, ultimately, in a fundamental sense, everything that I do is sort of...

53
00:06:03,847 --> 00:06:04,687
[SPEAKER_00]: I just hope for the best.

54
00:06:04,727 --> 00:06:05,588
[SPEAKER_00]: I hope for the best.

55
00:06:05,628 --> 00:06:06,869
[SPEAKER_00]: I do with my best of the abilities.

56
00:06:07,910 --> 00:06:09,892
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you think... why do you think they do that?

57
00:06:10,593 --> 00:06:11,874
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the practical basis?

58
00:06:12,134 --> 00:06:14,576
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is always a practical basis for every wrong view.

59
00:06:14,936 --> 00:06:20,461
[SPEAKER_00]: Practical, I mean, some sort of involvement that you try to... where you're not admitting to yourself.

60
00:06:21,022 --> 00:06:24,585
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in this particular case, what would the practical basis for that view be?

61
00:06:25,606 --> 00:06:33,052
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would somebody prefer to hold a view of, well, you can't really know anything or not... you can't be 100% sure, so...

62
00:06:35,075 --> 00:06:45,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when you start thinking that there might be the possibility, there is the possibility, there could be the possibility of direct knowledge, certainty, knowledge of right and wrong...

63
00:06:46,900 --> 00:06:49,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can't be really indifferent about that.

64
00:06:49,321 --> 00:06:49,601
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

65
00:06:49,621 --> 00:06:51,141
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to do something about it.

66
00:06:51,521 --> 00:06:53,642
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't... Or feel guilty for not doing it.

67
00:06:53,722 --> 00:06:53,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

68
00:06:54,022 --> 00:06:57,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, you can't cover up the weight, the weight of it.

69
00:06:57,543 --> 00:06:58,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's exactly it.

70
00:06:58,464 --> 00:07:03,585
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's always wrong views and inauthenticity, hand in hand, always.

71
00:07:03,605 --> 00:07:09,207
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot be authentic and hold the wrong views, nor can you remain inauthentic if you're dropping the wrong views.

72
00:07:09,547 --> 00:07:10,227
[SPEAKER_00]: That's impossible.

73
00:07:10,587 --> 00:07:10,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

74
00:07:11,448 --> 00:07:11,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

75
00:07:11,948 --> 00:07:12,308
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's...

76
00:07:14,493 --> 00:07:16,561
[SPEAKER_01]: I also found it interesting because...

77
00:07:20,377 --> 00:07:28,943
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, at some point, I used to think, I used to kind of not pay that much attention to the description of the wrong view that the Buddha was giving.

78
00:07:29,823 --> 00:07:39,550
[SPEAKER_01]: And, like, I've heard people say that it's just this sort of... some people in that time had these extreme views about the world.

79
00:07:39,610 --> 00:07:46,774
[SPEAKER_00]: It tends to be sort of, like, dumbed down and assumed it is like a... almost like a caricature examples.

80
00:07:47,935 --> 00:07:53,556
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, yeah, the eternalists and the annihilationists, we are past that point.

81
00:07:53,576 --> 00:07:57,697
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, no, no, those are the two fundamental existential views for as long as you're not a sotāpanna.

82
00:07:58,798 --> 00:08:01,198
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're going to be either eternalist or annihilationist.

83
00:08:01,418 --> 00:08:02,719
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no third option for you there.

84
00:08:03,439 --> 00:08:07,780
[SPEAKER_00]: All the variety of the views that Brahmajāla Sutta elaborates on fall into these two categories.

85
00:08:08,120 --> 00:08:09,560
[SPEAKER_01]: One says yes and one says no.

86
00:08:10,401 --> 00:08:11,281
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't see the middle.

87
00:08:13,481 --> 00:08:13,721
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

88
00:08:15,302 --> 00:08:15,522
[SPEAKER_01]: And...

89
00:08:18,410 --> 00:08:38,888
[SPEAKER_01]: At least, yeah, to me the the problem as well with kind of thinking that it's a, you know, these views, like the wrong view and the its counterpart as well, like the right view, are to be found in what somebody expressly, explicitly believes and professes, I think, such and such a thing.

90
00:08:41,930 --> 00:08:52,753
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it's a problem, because then it would mean that you just kind of get, let's say, the mundane, as it's called, right view or something like that, by sort of just adopting a particular set of ideas.

91
00:08:52,773 --> 00:09:10,257
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, like as far as the right view is a view, but obviously it's a view that goes in between the two kind of polar opposites of wrong views, yes and no, annihilationists, eternalists,

92
00:09:12,818 --> 00:09:16,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Mystics or scientifically oriented, it doesn't matter.

93
00:09:16,081 --> 00:09:21,727
[SPEAKER_00]: The right view is the view that puts first is first and everything else is second.

94
00:09:22,888 --> 00:09:28,413
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not directly opposed to the wrong views in a sense of left to the right.

95
00:09:29,314 --> 00:09:32,855
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like left-right are both wrong and this is above.

96
00:09:33,115 --> 00:09:34,235
[SPEAKER_00]: The right view is above both.

97
00:09:34,475 --> 00:09:46,577
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why going beyond the wrong views through a right view and eventually for an arahant who has brought the right view to, like, fulfillment, he has gone even beyond that vertical view of right perspective.

98
00:09:46,597 --> 00:09:48,017
[SPEAKER_00]: He has dropped everything in that sense.

99
00:09:49,998 --> 00:09:56,119
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, I think initially people will take the right view

100
00:09:57,412 --> 00:10:02,854
[SPEAKER_00]: In a wrong sense, through some sort of belief or faith, and that's inevitable.

101
00:10:03,735 --> 00:10:05,896
[SPEAKER_00]: But... and that in itself is not a problem.

102
00:10:05,936 --> 00:10:08,497
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is if then they think that's it.

103
00:10:08,757 --> 00:10:09,877
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all they needed to do.

104
00:10:10,137 --> 00:10:13,099
[SPEAKER_00]: Just let me assume the right things on the basis of information I collected.

105
00:10:13,499 --> 00:10:16,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Which, yes, it might be factually less wrong than other views you had before.

106
00:10:17,400 --> 00:10:22,783
[SPEAKER_00]: But the measure of the right view, it's not the measure of your belief, faith, confidence, certainty.

107
00:10:23,243 --> 00:10:25,924
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the measure of... it's measured by your inability to suffer.

108
00:10:27,247 --> 00:10:30,388
[SPEAKER_00]: So... and that's something you... you can't really lie.

109
00:10:30,628 --> 00:10:35,310
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you can obviously cover it up, but fundamentally you're always aware of it as a problem.

110
00:10:35,990 --> 00:10:38,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the fact that you're not free from suffering and you know it.

111
00:10:39,131 --> 00:10:44,793
[SPEAKER_00]: And if that's the case, means you can't... you shouldn't be deluding yourself into thinking, but I still have a right view.

112
00:10:44,953 --> 00:10:47,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you did, you wouldn't be afraid of suffering anymore.

113
00:10:49,261 --> 00:10:53,584
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't be affected by the suffering anymore.

114
00:10:54,664 --> 00:10:57,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe to some tiny degree, let's say like that.

115
00:10:57,546 --> 00:11:01,648
[SPEAKER_00]: From the arahant's point of view, yes, you would still be affected by suffering in sotāpanna.

116
00:11:01,908 --> 00:11:09,072
[SPEAKER_00]: But from puthujjana's point of view, well, you're pretty much on the level of arahantship, as far as puthujjana is concerned, because he has no knowledge of any escape from suffering.

117
00:11:10,653 --> 00:11:12,454
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the fundamental difference.

118
00:11:13,994 --> 00:11:14,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Can somebody who

119
00:11:18,658 --> 00:11:32,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Without knowing it, has wrong views underlying their actions and their... what they're doing, can they practice the dhamma?

120
00:11:35,544 --> 00:11:38,044
[SPEAKER_00]: You mean the actual... the actual dhamma?

121
00:11:38,124 --> 00:11:38,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

122
00:11:38,465 --> 00:11:39,805
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, that's impossible.

123
00:11:41,217 --> 00:11:45,082
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if they, let's say, try to be very virtuous... Yeah.

124
00:11:45,703 --> 00:11:51,551
[SPEAKER_00]: All that's commendable and to be encouraged, but it's the right view that makes dhamma visible.

125
00:11:52,893 --> 00:11:56,558
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you're not seeing what you're supposed to be doing, then you aren't doing it.

126
00:11:58,017 --> 00:12:02,942
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if you're going through all the right motions, that's when you realize to say, oh, he's doing it for the wrong reasons.

127
00:12:03,203 --> 00:12:04,984
[SPEAKER_00]: He's doing right things for the wrong reasons.

128
00:12:05,805 --> 00:12:12,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's still better than doing wrong things for the wrong reasons, but fundamentally, the dhamma only arises with the right view.

129
00:12:13,733 --> 00:12:15,415
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, it's important to just

130
00:12:16,932 --> 00:12:28,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Do not lie to yourself about it, because if you think you have access to dhamma, the actual dhamma, the actual path to liberation, the Buddha's teaching, without being a sotāpanna, that's it.

131
00:12:28,937 --> 00:12:36,440
[SPEAKER_00]: You already think... you already basically assume that you have gained the access to the result of your right view.

132
00:12:37,320 --> 00:12:48,038
[SPEAKER_00]: Without the right view, which means you're not gonna recognize the importance and the severity of having wrong views and the importance of actually needing to get the right view, because there is no dhamma below that.

133
00:12:49,361 --> 00:12:53,322
[SPEAKER_00]: And people would ignore the importance and would not make the effort for the same reason we said in the beginning.

134
00:12:53,462 --> 00:12:59,604
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, there's nothing can be... you can't be sure, because that would then make them, like, too unpleasant, feel too unpleasantly about it.

135
00:12:59,744 --> 00:13:10,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it is... it is a serious weight, a burden, and especially if you don't know what to do about it, and now you're even more overwhelmed with the confusion, it's like, well, that's at least a good starting point.

136
00:13:10,567 --> 00:13:12,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Now at least you know what work needs to be done.

137
00:13:14,128 --> 00:13:15,888
[SPEAKER_01]: Now at least you realize the...

138
00:13:20,598 --> 00:13:22,179
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a problem with the position you're in.

139
00:13:22,619 --> 00:13:23,500
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

140
00:13:24,100 --> 00:13:29,183
[SPEAKER_00]: And in order to recognize the... Well, in order to find a solution for a problem, you first need to stop ignoring it.

141
00:13:29,304 --> 00:13:36,328
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean... And you know that in a mundane, day-to-day life, when everybody is aware that this person has a problem, but that person is refusing to admit it.

142
00:13:36,688 --> 00:13:45,714
[SPEAKER_00]: And nothing you do, nothing you offer, nothing you... in a form of help, will actually help, because they refuse to acknowledge that there is a problem.

143
00:13:46,455 --> 00:13:48,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Blatantly lying about it to themselves.

144
00:13:49,550 --> 00:13:51,533
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the same on the level of the mind.

145
00:13:51,553 --> 00:13:57,941
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unless you recognize that, no, no, no, this is a factual problem, that the only way out of this problem would be to get the right view.

146
00:13:59,222 --> 00:14:02,567
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you take it seriously and then you actually have a chance of getting the right view.

147
00:14:07,893 --> 00:14:08,114
[SPEAKER_01]: And...

148
00:14:13,656 --> 00:14:14,918
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what we were saying before.

149
00:14:14,958 --> 00:14:22,627
[SPEAKER_00]: The prerequisite for the right view is the right honesty, or self-honesty, or transparency, or authenticity, whatever you want to call it.

150
00:14:23,328 --> 00:14:24,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Without it, it doesn't matter how much.

151
00:14:25,071 --> 00:14:26,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Fifty years you can practice dhamma.

152
00:14:26,432 --> 00:14:27,434
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not practicing the dhamma.

153
00:14:31,330 --> 00:14:42,742
[SPEAKER_01]: And can you get the right view by doing something other than questioning your views and trying to undo or like seeing how the wrong view is wrong?

154
00:14:42,922 --> 00:14:43,523
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

155
00:14:43,763 --> 00:14:47,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Because whatever you would be doing in that case would be on the basis of your wrong views.

156
00:14:48,729 --> 00:14:51,211
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not really uprooting or undoing the problem.

157
00:14:51,331 --> 00:14:52,813
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just building on top of it.

158
00:14:53,434 --> 00:14:53,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

159
00:14:56,697 --> 00:14:56,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

160
00:14:57,597 --> 00:15:24,431
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just another branch on the same topic that is related to what you just said, that I've kind of come up against, is that when people start to practice, and now they say, okay, I want to be a sotāpanna, and if I'm not a sotāpanna, then I can't say that I know.

161
00:15:26,829 --> 00:15:33,620
[SPEAKER_01]: But then... so then, they're kind of... the problem is that...

162
00:15:36,178 --> 00:15:43,644
[SPEAKER_01]: That people can sort of say, well, there is no certainty, apart from one who is a sotāpanna.

163
00:15:44,685 --> 00:15:52,952
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, they kind of don't actually change their attitude from that basic attitude of saying, oh, well, nothing can be certain, because they're kind of thinking like...

164
00:15:52,972 --> 00:15:59,017
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they abstractly allow possibility of being certain as a sotāpanna, but practically, they absolutely abolish it.

165
00:15:59,297 --> 00:15:59,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

166
00:15:59,677 --> 00:16:04,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's... that's quite... that's probably the most common kind of attitude between, like, lay people and the monks.

167
00:16:05,433 --> 00:16:07,819
[SPEAKER_00]: In a sense, you're all striving... they're all striving for something.

168
00:16:08,747 --> 00:16:26,574
[SPEAKER_00]: But, practically, if, you know, if you were to develop clarity about it, to make the effort, or to investigate the meaning, and so on, to actually start doing something about the lack of clarity which is preventing you from bringing something about, that's always dismissed as if, like, wow, that's just your views and opinions, like, well, everything, that's your view and opinion.

169
00:16:27,174 --> 00:16:32,076
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the question is not having any views and opinions in that kind of non-committal sense, because you're committing to that.

170
00:16:32,576 --> 00:16:36,678
[SPEAKER_00]: The question is, yeah, undoing all the wrong views by seeing how they're wrong, why they're wrong.

171
00:16:37,721 --> 00:16:42,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Finding contradiction in them and that's how you will be unable to hold them and that's how you are getting closer to the right view.

172
00:16:42,585 --> 00:16:46,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognizing where the problem is and not covering it up anymore.

173
00:16:47,593 --> 00:16:48,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

174
00:16:48,173 --> 00:17:01,117
[SPEAKER_01]: And kind of a version of that too is saying like, okay, I think X, Y and Z, but I'm not a sotāpanna, so I might be wrong or I'm probably wrong in some way.

175
00:17:01,157 --> 00:17:05,639
[SPEAKER_01]: So then I'll ask somebody else who I think is a sotāpanna and then I'll follow what they say.

176
00:17:05,659 --> 00:17:06,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Or not even that.

177
00:17:06,279 --> 00:17:11,641
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll ask somebody else who might have an opinion about it and that somehow, you know, we're going to arrive at the right conclusion.

178
00:17:11,881 --> 00:17:12,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

179
00:17:13,344 --> 00:17:15,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I heard it, I don't know how many times, exactly that.

180
00:17:15,785 --> 00:17:27,252
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, somebody would ask a very clear question, practical question, and then the answer they would get would be quoting what somebody else might say in that instance.

181
00:17:27,372 --> 00:17:31,175
[SPEAKER_00]: I was like, the only... again, in a mundane sense.

182
00:17:32,227 --> 00:17:44,056
[SPEAKER_00]: The only time I would give you an answer in that way, like you ask me something and I say, well, there's this such and such, and he likes to do this in such cases, is because I don't have a clue what I'm talking about.

183
00:17:44,776 --> 00:17:51,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if I understood what I'm talking about, I would understand your question, and I would understand the best answer for you.

184
00:17:52,342 --> 00:17:55,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if I'm absolutely kind of illiterate in every other way.

185
00:17:55,504 --> 00:17:59,007
[SPEAKER_00]: You understand the craft, you understand the way out, you will know how to convey it.

186
00:18:00,266 --> 00:18:19,874
[SPEAKER_00]: And the clear indication of not knowing the way out, when somebody asks you a question about suffering or dhamma, and you just keep sort of throwing in references or referring to this, not in a kind of, I'll give you a reference to help what I just told you, but more like, oh yes, you know, the big senior Ajahn, he always says this.

187
00:18:19,894 --> 00:18:22,976
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, yeah, but I didn't ask the big senior Ajahn, I'm asking you.

188
00:18:23,756 --> 00:18:25,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, you offered yourself for that purpose.

189
00:18:26,577 --> 00:18:27,918
[SPEAKER_00]: So that should always be a red flag.

190
00:18:29,473 --> 00:18:30,974
[SPEAKER_00]: And I remember that was always the thing as well.

191
00:18:30,994 --> 00:18:38,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I personally kind of, from the start, was, you know, reading the suttas, philosophy, nirvīra, that was my thing.

192
00:18:39,116 --> 00:18:40,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Never really listened to any dhamma talks and so on.

193
00:18:41,297 --> 00:18:44,198
[SPEAKER_00]: And first time I heard dhamma talks, I thought...

194
00:18:45,482 --> 00:18:48,125
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I felt like there was nothing being said.

195
00:18:48,185 --> 00:18:51,228
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just people talking for an hour, giving these generic ideas.

196
00:18:51,628 --> 00:18:54,371
[SPEAKER_00]: And I thought, oh, maybe that's just the form of the dhamma talk.

197
00:18:54,391 --> 00:18:56,012
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't really be very specific.

198
00:18:56,172 --> 00:19:01,878
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, the more I listened, the more it became apparent that, no, you can be specific, it's just that these people aren't.

199
00:19:02,438 --> 00:19:07,303
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only reason they are not specific is because either they don't want to tell you, which... why wouldn't they do that?

200
00:19:08,064 --> 00:19:09,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Or they don't know what to tell you.

201
00:19:09,990 --> 00:19:19,954
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just let me fill an hour with these inspirational terms and so on, and kind of resembling the suttas, sometimes more, sometimes less, but overall, if you'd know what you're talking about, you would have a straight answer.

202
00:19:20,594 --> 00:19:28,438
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's a direct sutta exactly that says that, when the king, or whoever he was, asked the Buddha, do you prepare the answers beforehand?

203
00:19:28,458 --> 00:19:28,918
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, no.

204
00:19:29,478 --> 00:19:30,718
[SPEAKER_00]: You are the King Master Chariot.

205
00:19:30,878 --> 00:19:31,739
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, yes, I am.

206
00:19:32,299 --> 00:19:37,841
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody were to ask you anything about the workings of a chariot, would you have to prepare an answer?

207
00:19:37,861 --> 00:19:38,201
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, no.

208
00:19:38,221 --> 00:19:43,162
[SPEAKER_00]: If they wake me in the middle of the night, I'll give them an answer immediately, because I fully understood the working of it.

209
00:19:43,922 --> 00:19:49,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with the one who has basically practiced the way rightly, who is enlightened or who is on the way to be enlightened.

210
00:19:49,124 --> 00:19:52,105
[SPEAKER_00]: He will be able to convey their way at any given time.

211
00:19:53,939 --> 00:19:59,784
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it was always like a... and then, obviously, you know, once I started talking, I realized, oh, yeah, no, you can be very specific.

212
00:19:59,804 --> 00:20:01,986
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as a matter of fact, the suttas were talks.

213
00:20:02,546 --> 00:20:05,869
[SPEAKER_00]: The suttas, you know, the Buddha didn't say and write... wrote down the suttas.

214
00:20:05,889 --> 00:20:09,993
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't sit down and wrote down the suttas, you know, premeditating what he's gonna say.

215
00:20:10,193 --> 00:20:12,035
[SPEAKER_00]: It was basically recorded conversations.

216
00:20:12,215 --> 00:20:16,318
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is no problem with the medium of... of, like, a direct discourse.

217
00:20:16,418 --> 00:20:19,881
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is that there is no substance to it, generally.

218
00:20:20,642 --> 00:20:24,364
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's like, again, that's found in suttas probably often over read.

219
00:20:24,404 --> 00:20:34,568
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like when when a man comes seeking heartwood, and ask a question how to find the heartwood, and then he was given the answer about sapwood.

220
00:20:35,829 --> 00:20:41,271
[SPEAKER_00]: And him, the man not knowing any better, he takes the sapwood to be the heartwood, and he stopped looking for the heartwood.

221
00:20:43,772 --> 00:20:43,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

222
00:20:45,933 --> 00:20:46,213
[SPEAKER_01]: And...

223
00:20:48,474 --> 00:20:50,015
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, to...

224
00:20:53,650 --> 00:21:04,535
[SPEAKER_01]: So, another problem, I think, also, is like when somebody needs to rely on somebody else's views, or what somebody else says, because they themselves don't know.

225
00:21:04,555 --> 00:21:05,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

226
00:21:06,656 --> 00:21:16,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, what can happen is that they themselves stop taking responsibility for having, like, okay, so-and-so said this, I have faith in him, so that must be right.

227
00:21:16,260 --> 00:21:16,700
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

228
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,461
[SPEAKER_01]: And now you... it's not me thinking this anymore.

229
00:21:19,661 --> 00:21:21,422
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not what I think anymore.

230
00:21:22,062 --> 00:21:22,662
[SPEAKER_01]: And now...

231
00:21:22,682 --> 00:21:25,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if that person is actual Buddha himself.

232
00:21:25,664 --> 00:21:25,964
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

233
00:21:26,664 --> 00:21:32,067
[SPEAKER_00]: If you took the practice on that level, means there is no practice for you, for as long as that doesn't change, that attitude.

234
00:21:32,707 --> 00:21:33,007
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

235
00:21:34,608 --> 00:21:41,251
[SPEAKER_01]: And... and so then, like, you know, it's terrible.

236
00:21:41,872 --> 00:21:46,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything that contradicts what you have decided to have faith in,

237
00:21:47,445 --> 00:21:58,539
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say, oh, no, no, no, that... that can't be right, while at the same time kind of forgetting that actually you don't know for yourself which one is... Well, that's... that's like one of the...

238
00:21:59,789 --> 00:22:01,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the basic logical fallacies.

239
00:22:01,871 --> 00:22:03,952
[SPEAKER_00]: And all of them have the same thread.

240
00:22:04,892 --> 00:22:07,274
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know if I mentioned it, but it's all that cover-up.

241
00:22:07,574 --> 00:22:09,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Avoiding to take responsibility.

242
00:22:09,695 --> 00:22:18,161
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's either you assume the authority, either some senior monk or your teacher or the religion or the texts or, you know, the scholarly study of the dictionary.

243
00:22:18,201 --> 00:22:19,061
[SPEAKER_00]: See, now that's it.

244
00:22:19,101 --> 00:22:20,882
[SPEAKER_00]: That's factual, how it's written.

245
00:22:21,703 --> 00:22:24,385
[SPEAKER_00]: All done for the purpose of, yeah, but what does that mean for you?

246
00:22:24,685 --> 00:22:27,867
[SPEAKER_00]: Just like the difference that the Buddha often talks about the sort of the phrasing and the meaning.

247
00:22:28,873 --> 00:22:32,884
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the meaning you won't find in the phrasing, like, self-evident.

248
00:22:33,877 --> 00:22:43,821
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to actually undo your wrong views for these subtler meanings to become... because everybody reads the suttas, but almost nobody gets the right view on account of reading the suttas.

249
00:22:43,881 --> 00:22:44,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

250
00:22:44,462 --> 00:23:00,929
[SPEAKER_00]: Either the suttas are wrong, or the instruments that people are reading the suttas with, the views, what they perceive, the concepts, everything else implied in it, is completely covered, like layers and layers and layers of... of, yeah, the wrong views that the Buddha lists in the Brahmajala Sutta.

251
00:23:01,903 --> 00:23:05,027
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why there is no meaning becoming a parent.

252
00:23:10,674 --> 00:23:20,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Does it kind of maybe come down to, you know, the difference between, okay, is my aim to

253
00:23:22,215 --> 00:23:27,120
[SPEAKER_01]: That's going to be the first thing.

254
00:23:27,560 --> 00:23:29,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I want to become a sotāpanna.

255
00:23:29,142 --> 00:23:32,185
[SPEAKER_01]: As the suttas say, such and such became a sotāpanna.

256
00:23:32,225 --> 00:23:33,386
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if he can do it, I can do it.

257
00:23:33,406 --> 00:23:33,987
[SPEAKER_00]: That type of thing.

258
00:23:40,595 --> 00:23:46,316
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, it's taken wrongly, but it does sort of aim, in the broadest sense, in the right direction.

259
00:23:46,916 --> 00:23:58,479
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, yes, you could spend next, as I said, five decades of just protecting that view and not really recognizing or stepping further into understanding what actually means to be a sotāpanna.

260
00:23:59,039 --> 00:24:02,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I want to become sotāpanna and I'm doing all these things, but why do you do what you're doing?

261
00:24:02,860 --> 00:24:04,020
[SPEAKER_00]: Why are you doing these practices?

262
00:24:04,261 --> 00:24:05,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Why are you even keeping the virtue?

263
00:24:05,941 --> 00:24:09,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, do you understand or you're just doing all of that because you were told to?

264
00:24:11,122 --> 00:24:12,403
[SPEAKER_00]: And that would be the difference then.

265
00:24:12,523 --> 00:24:18,626
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody's actually getting closer to become a Sotāpanna, he's the one who starts questioning all these things and trying to understand them.

266
00:24:19,807 --> 00:24:27,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, inevitably, you're going to arrive at the doubt and... understanding what the actual problem of the doubt is.

267
00:24:27,291 --> 00:24:28,612
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not what you're doubting.

268
00:24:29,352 --> 00:24:31,573
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the presence of the discomfort of the doubt.

269
00:24:32,934 --> 00:24:37,917
[SPEAKER_00]: And, again, that's something you can theorize about it, but it will never be right until you actually start enduring it.

270
00:24:43,655 --> 00:24:57,084
[SPEAKER_01]: And... yeah, because people can kind of think, well, okay, being, becoming a Sotāpanna will free me from doubt, rather than it being...

271
00:24:57,284 --> 00:24:59,286
[SPEAKER_00]: Freeing myself from doubt will make me a Sotāpanna.

272
00:24:59,366 --> 00:25:00,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Yep, that's exactly it.

273
00:25:01,367 --> 00:25:02,448
[SPEAKER_00]: The horse or the cart.

274
00:25:03,208 --> 00:25:12,695
[SPEAKER_00]: People put the cart in front of the horse and think, oh, let me just do these practices, they will result in my magical awakening of Sotāpatti, and then these fetters will fall off.

275
00:25:14,117 --> 00:25:18,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Self-view, doubt, and virtue and duty, and so on.

276
00:25:18,859 --> 00:25:20,040
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's the other way around.

277
00:25:20,740 --> 00:25:25,402
[SPEAKER_00]: It's you fully understanding these fetters, is how you become a sotāpanna.

278
00:25:26,302 --> 00:25:37,587
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you understanding the virtue and duty, and freeing yourself from that assumption of it, freeing yourself from assuming the external sense of self outside of your experience, and freeing yourself from the pressure of the doubt.

279
00:25:39,587 --> 00:25:45,529
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when these three fetters don't move you, it's safe to say you're a sattvāna, not other way around.

280
00:25:52,192 --> 00:26:00,255
[SPEAKER_01]: It's interesting how, even like the ideas that people have about the dhamma,

281
00:26:01,357 --> 00:26:07,210
[SPEAKER_01]: It can be used to sort of justify holding the wrong views.

282
00:26:07,771 --> 00:26:09,815
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm thinking of an example where...

283
00:26:11,110 --> 00:26:14,251
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, somebody might say, okay, no, this is how it is.

284
00:26:14,291 --> 00:26:17,333
[SPEAKER_01]: This is how it can be discerned directly in your experience.

285
00:26:18,093 --> 00:26:27,937
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the response was, well, no, because, you know, that's not a ground for being sure about it, because, you know, a Pūrtajana sees self in his experience.

286
00:26:27,957 --> 00:26:31,979
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, the idea that there is... there's not supposed to be a self,

287
00:26:33,099 --> 00:26:33,940
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right, right.

288
00:26:34,800 --> 00:26:37,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's it's it's it's on that kind of arm's-length level.

289
00:26:38,361 --> 00:26:39,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, where do people...

290
00:26:39,201 --> 00:26:52,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, it's false, but it's it's it's never taken on that personal level, because, again, it's gonna... fetters are not seen, because people avoid seeing them, or admitting them, because they're unpleasant.

291
00:26:53,858 --> 00:27:06,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, the reason why everybody so easily assumes external duty and virtue and practice and method and technique is because it's easier than to leave it non-specific and then feel the burden of confusion and everything.

292
00:27:06,776 --> 00:27:07,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I doing right?

293
00:27:07,237 --> 00:27:07,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I doing wrong?

294
00:27:07,817 --> 00:27:08,198
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I do?

295
00:27:08,218 --> 00:27:08,699
[SPEAKER_00]: How do I do it?

296
00:27:09,119 --> 00:27:09,279
[SPEAKER_00]: Or...

297
00:27:09,780 --> 00:27:18,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Fundamentally, if you stay in that state, you would recognize, oh, my only concern here, with this entire state, is that it's unpleasant and I refuse to accept that discomfort of it.

298
00:27:18,868 --> 00:27:20,410
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I'm worried about what to do.

299
00:27:20,530 --> 00:27:22,151
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because I really need to know what to do.

300
00:27:22,471 --> 00:27:24,453
[SPEAKER_00]: And more often than not, you actually know what you need to do.

301
00:27:25,114 --> 00:27:31,359
[SPEAKER_00]: But if with the pain pressures you, you doubt everything, because your primary concern is aversion to its pain must be avoided, always.

302
00:27:31,399 --> 00:27:34,262
[SPEAKER_00]: That pain must be, you know, dealt with instantly.

303
00:27:36,108 --> 00:27:40,172
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you get then liable to doubt and everything else, because you gave it power.

304
00:27:46,128 --> 00:27:54,275
[SPEAKER_01]: And not even seeing that, you know, what you're doing is trying to avoid the pain of it, because your mind is focused on, like, to solve this, you know, question and this problem.

305
00:27:55,436 --> 00:28:07,146
[SPEAKER_01]: And related to that, actually, like, another another thing I noticed about this kind of thing that people end up coming down to, as, oh, well, you know, I can't be sure about that, or that you can't really be certain about this thing, is that

306
00:28:08,187 --> 00:28:27,673
[SPEAKER_01]: It can be kind of used to justify holding... let's say you have an idea about right and wrong, for example, like usually... and this... and not to go into kind of specific ideas of right and wrong, but it usually would kind of be like on the level of a

307
00:28:29,260 --> 00:28:40,344
[SPEAKER_01]: A specific template of how the world should look, and how people should behave, and should act, and it should be like this, and etc., that this is right, and then anything that's...

308
00:28:40,744 --> 00:28:48,307
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you kind of... it come... you come to the point of realizing, well, that's... nothing I do will ever be able to create that template how I want it to be.

309
00:28:48,347 --> 00:28:49,348
[SPEAKER_01]: It can't be quite consistent.

310
00:28:49,368 --> 00:28:50,328
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always out of reach in that sense.

311
00:28:50,348 --> 00:28:50,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

312
00:28:51,529 --> 00:28:54,910
[SPEAKER_01]: And instead of then realizing all my idea of what is

313
00:28:55,573 --> 00:28:58,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Right and wrong might be flawed or might be wrong.

314
00:28:58,136 --> 00:28:58,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

315
00:28:58,637 --> 00:29:01,921
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, oh, well, you can't ever be perfect.

316
00:29:02,041 --> 00:29:02,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yeah.

317
00:29:02,943 --> 00:29:04,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Just try your best and that's it.

318
00:29:05,185 --> 00:29:09,311
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, even that then becomes just a coping, coping mechanism.

319
00:29:10,515 --> 00:29:25,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you realize you are trying your best, and still nothing changes in a way, things don't matter, then you either just dismiss that, or you reckon as well, maybe, you know, my best efforts are completely misdirected.

320
00:29:27,348 --> 00:29:29,849
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the painful thing, where the honesty comes in.

321
00:29:29,910 --> 00:29:36,554
[SPEAKER_00]: If you start admitting it, you realize, yeah, because if my... if tenth of these efforts that I have been making factually, were actually directed to the things

322
00:29:37,771 --> 00:29:50,794
[SPEAKER_00]: The matter, such as freedom from suffering, greed, aversion, delusion, if you made effort to clarify that and not act out of it and everything else we talk about, you realize, oh, yeah, I would have had the results I'm hoping for.

323
00:29:50,874 --> 00:30:00,316
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, it's... People are easily misled because they already are misleading themselves through not being honest enough.

324
00:30:01,096 --> 00:30:03,377
[SPEAKER_00]: Why are you doing what you're doing?

325
00:30:03,537 --> 00:30:05,097
[SPEAKER_00]: And why are you holding views that you're holding?

326
00:30:09,850 --> 00:30:21,618
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's much easier instead of... that is so unpleasant to kind of accept the possibility that everything that I've been doing and all the efforts I've been making might have been in the wrong direction or perhaps... Yeah.

327
00:30:21,718 --> 00:30:27,162
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's... it's so... it's much easier then to kind of, yeah, say... fall back on this.

328
00:30:28,543 --> 00:30:32,286
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, it can never be quite perfect and I... nothing I do...

329
00:30:34,005 --> 00:30:38,408
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, like, there are no fruits and results, ultimately, of good and bad actions.

330
00:30:38,428 --> 00:30:39,069
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

331
00:30:39,249 --> 00:30:42,331
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you fail to even grasp the mundane right view.

332
00:30:43,572 --> 00:30:51,658
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly, I mean, I've seen senior monks saying that after, you know, I don't know, I said, decades in rope, giving dhamma talks and saying like, no, no, no.

333
00:30:52,038 --> 00:30:53,699
[SPEAKER_00]: And I don't mean they were speaking poetically.

334
00:30:53,879 --> 00:30:55,841
[SPEAKER_00]: They spent an hour explaining what they meant.

335
00:30:55,981 --> 00:30:58,843
[SPEAKER_00]: And what they were saying was, oh, it's nothing to be attained.

336
00:31:00,307 --> 00:31:03,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, all these things about, you know, getting... No, no, no.

337
00:31:03,509 --> 00:31:05,211
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not about attaining anything.

338
00:31:05,311 --> 00:31:07,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't worry about becoming a Sotāpanna.

339
00:31:07,873 --> 00:31:08,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't worry about it.

340
00:31:08,853 --> 00:31:10,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Just, you know, be here now.

341
00:31:10,675 --> 00:31:16,639
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the whole thing, the whole talk revolves around that concept of I don't do anything, just accept yourself the way you are.

342
00:31:16,819 --> 00:31:17,119
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like...

343
00:31:17,700 --> 00:31:18,580
[SPEAKER_00]: But you are a patujana.

344
00:31:19,941 --> 00:31:24,665
[SPEAKER_00]: If you accept yourself as somebody who is drowning, means you will never gonna save yourself.

345
00:31:24,765 --> 00:31:25,385
[SPEAKER_00]: You will drown.

346
00:31:25,846 --> 00:31:27,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, I accept the fact that I'm drowning.

347
00:31:27,487 --> 00:31:27,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, fine.

348
00:31:28,207 --> 00:31:28,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Goodbye then.

349
00:31:29,873 --> 00:31:31,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, no, I don't accept the fact I'm drowning.

350
00:31:31,514 --> 00:31:35,215
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm gonna at least try and get out of this this quicksand or whatever I'm in.

351
00:31:36,036 --> 00:31:37,976
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, now you at least have a chance, because you're trying.

352
00:31:39,457 --> 00:31:43,899
[SPEAKER_00]: But I'm sure that none of those those those senior teachers started with that view.

353
00:31:44,779 --> 00:31:49,681
[SPEAKER_00]: They probably had more or less genuine view in the beginning, but having been going wrongly about it,

354
00:31:50,490 --> 00:31:52,433
[SPEAKER_00]: You end up covering it up more and more and more.

355
00:31:52,573 --> 00:32:00,003
[SPEAKER_00]: And obviously, the more you go in that direction, the further away you drift from even basic honesty on that existential level.

356
00:32:00,944 --> 00:32:07,192
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, eh... it becomes easier to then even just blatantly cover up or dismiss or...

357
00:32:07,833 --> 00:32:11,416
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, not even consider whether that might contradict the suttas and so on.

358
00:32:11,616 --> 00:32:15,899
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, well, you know, look, I've been 50 years doing this and yeah, I'm fine.

359
00:32:16,139 --> 00:32:17,000
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm a good person.

360
00:32:17,460 --> 00:32:18,621
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not about being good person.

361
00:32:18,661 --> 00:32:19,982
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about ceasing to be a person.

362
00:32:20,002 --> 00:32:22,004
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about freeing yourself from entire saṃsara.

363
00:32:22,624 --> 00:32:26,647
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to be a good person, fine, but that's not what the dhamma teaches.

364
00:32:27,047 --> 00:32:30,710
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like somebody who can't practice the dhamma, okay, at least be a good person.

365
00:32:31,791 --> 00:32:34,333
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you can practice the dhamma, that is the goal.

366
00:32:35,883 --> 00:32:53,931
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, people come and look up to those, you know, teachers or teachings to find the heartwood, to find the source of the meaning, the core of the teaching, and all they get is kind of irrelevant bark and sapwood and stuff that's useless.

367
00:32:54,552 --> 00:32:59,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Or it's just relatively useful for mundane matters.

368
00:33:03,660 --> 00:33:10,023
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there was nothing to be attained, would have the Buddha talked about it so often.

369
00:33:11,944 --> 00:33:18,367
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's all about just being here and now and accepting, you know, not judging and whatever else, the Buddha would have surely mentioned that.

370
00:33:19,522 --> 00:33:19,922
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

371
00:33:20,242 --> 00:33:33,925
[SPEAKER_00]: He does not tolerate, he doesn't welcome, he doesn't act out, he creates his thesis and endures it, endures the elements, endures the words of others, purifies his mind from the states, day and night, takes responsibilities, virtues, sense restraint, doesn't look for loopholes.

372
00:33:35,126 --> 00:33:36,106
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's plenty to do.

373
00:33:36,966 --> 00:33:44,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, your conclusion from all of that is, right, right, okay, be here now and just relax and, you know, calm down and that's it.

374
00:33:45,108 --> 00:33:45,668
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all fine.

375
00:33:45,788 --> 00:33:46,428
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no problem.

376
00:33:46,468 --> 00:33:47,729
[SPEAKER_00]: You are the one creating problems.

377
00:33:48,569 --> 00:33:48,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

378
00:33:49,830 --> 00:33:54,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Because problems are there, it can't seem that you are creating them by looking at them.

379
00:33:54,874 --> 00:34:02,259
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what the child does when it's scared, it doesn't want to deal with it, it covers its eyes, thinking, okay, I don't see it, it's not there anymore.

380
00:34:03,580 --> 00:34:06,282
[SPEAKER_00]: But if it were not there, why would you have to cover your eyes in the beginning?

